Price accuracy
- prices.csv values are eBay sold prices. Local flip prices may be lower or higher. Refresh the data every 1-2 weeks.
- coolers, PSUs, storage and cases have no price data, so they count as $0 until you type a value on the evaluate page.
  Desktop and combo profit is understated by their value. Collecting sold prices for them would fix it.
- prebuilt desktops (Dell OptiPlex, HP, Lenovo) never have an identifiable board, so the board counts as $0
- the desktop search (179) is mostly prebuilt office PCs, all-in-ones and Raspberry Pis. Their titles say "8GB RAM"
  without DDR type, so RAM isn't found either. Few deals will come from it as long as it's valued by parts.
- "16GB DDR4" with no kit layout is read as 1x16GB, which prices a bit lower than 2x8GB

sold_parser.py extractors (found while testing; scanner works around some of these)
- extract_mobo needs a brand in the title. scanner.mobo_by_chipset now prices "B660M Motherboard" as an average B660M board.
- extract_mobo keeps words like "combo" and "bundle" in the model name. scanner.clean_title strips them.
- extract_mobo drops words before a dashed chipset: "ASUS TUF GAMING Z490-PLUS" becomes "ASUS Z490-PLUS",
  which misses the exact price and uses the chipset fallback
- extract_ram reads GPU memory as RAM ("RTX 3070 8GB 32GB DDR4" -> 8GB) and extract_mobo picks up storage words ("1TB").
  scanner.clean_title strips these before extracting; fixing the extractors would be cleaner.

